            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Talk about everyday objects and how they are used. Interactive: Express preferences about familiar topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can talk about everyday objects at the store and simply explain how they are used (e.g., "I use a basket to carry food."). Can express preferences for shopping items (e.g., "I like apples."). Can describe basic items at the store using simple words and phrases (e.g., "Apples are expensive.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts a "Fruit Basket Count" game. The teacher holds up flashcards of various fruits (pineapple, apple, orange, banana) and asks students to identify them and count them aloud. "What is this? How many apples do you see?" (e.g., "One apple!"). This activates prior vocabulary and numbers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the target phrases by pretending to shop. Teacher holds up a flashcard (e.g., 3 bananas) and says clearly: "I want three bananas, please." Then holds up another (e.g., 5 pineapples) and says: "I want five pineapples, please." The teacher emphasizes the polite "please" and the pronunciation of /b/ in "banana" and /p/ in "pineapple", and blends numbers like /ˈs-ɪ-k-s/ for six. Students repeat chorally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the phrases and politeness.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a picture of an apple. "If I want this apple, do I say 'I want apple' or 'I want ONE apple, please'?" (Expected: "One apple, please!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "Do we say 'please' when we ask for something? Is it polite?" (Expected: "Yes, it's polite!")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Let's Go Shopping!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated dialogue projected on the board or printed as a handout, depicting a customer at a fruit stall in a market. The dialogue will feature a simple exchange requesting fruit, e.g., "Customer: Hello! I want three apples, please. Seller: Here you go! Customer: Thank you! How much is one pineapple? Seller: It's ten dollars. Customer: Oh, that's expensive! I want five oranges instead."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand and express quantity, make polite requests for items, and express simple preferences about shopping items using previously introduced vocabulary (pineapple, apple, orange, banana, money, price, market, cheap, expensive) and grammar (Number + noun, Present simple "I want...", "How much is...?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Oral response):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "Where are the people in the picture?" (Expected: "At the market!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> "How many apples does the customer want?" (Expected: "Three apples!") "Does the customer buy the pineapple or the oranges?" (Expected: "Oranges!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Oral):</b> "Are the apples cheap?" (Teacher points to dialogue info for confirmation, e.g., "Yes, they are cheap!" or "No, they are expensive!")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice expressing quantity (Number + noun, numbers 1-10), making polite requests ("I want three apples, please.", "Can I have two bananas, please?"), and stating preferences ("I like apples."). Focus on correct pronunciation of /b/, /p/, and phonemic blending for numbers and 'money' /ˈm-ʌ-n-i/.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Fill the Basket" Oral Cloze:</b> The teacher displays a large picture of an empty shopping basket and several fruit flashcards with different quantities. The teacher points to a flashcard (e.g., 4 oranges) and initiates a sentence: "I want four...". Students complete the sentence orally in chorus and individually: "...oranges, please!" Teacher provides feedback on pronunciation and quantity. Repeat with various items like "three bananas", "one pineapple", "ten apples".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "Shopping List Substitution Drill":</b> The teacher provides a sentence frame on the board: "I want [NUMBER] [FRUIT], please." Students work in pairs. The teacher calls out a number and a fruit (e.g., "Two, bananas!"). Students orally substitute the words into the frame. Partner A says: "I want two bananas, please." Partner B confirms. Then switch roles. Teacher monitors for correct number usage and politeness.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: "Ask and Answer":</b> Students receive small picture cards of fruit with quantities (e.g., 3 apples, 5 pineapples, 2 oranges). In pairs, Student A holds up a card (e.g., 3 apples) and asks: "Can I have three apples, please?" Student B responds: "Yes, here you go!" or "Sorry, no apples." Then they swap roles. Teacher encourages natural speech and polite expressions.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Let's Go Shopping!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Market Role-Play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Description:</b> Students will work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group sets up a mini 'market stall' using realia or fruit flashcards on their desks. One student is the "seller" (cashier), and the others are "customers." Customers take turns approaching the stall and requesting specific quantities of items using "I want [NUMBER] [FRUIT], please" or "Can I have [NUMBER] [FRUIT], please?". The seller responds and can also describe the items (e.g., "These apples are fresh and sweet!" or "Pineapples are expensive today."). Students should also practice saying "Thank you!" and "You're welcome!" The focus is on fluency, interaction, and completing the transaction.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> This activity directly simulates a shopping experience at a market, allowing students to use all target language related to requesting and describing fruit and quantity within the "Let's Go Shopping!" scenario and "I Want Five Pineapples" theme.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Market Role-Play" in their groups. The teacher circulates and observes each group, ticking off a checklist for individual student performance. Students also use a simple peer assessment sheet to evaluate their partners.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher & Peer):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially /b/, /p/, and blended numbers).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (fruit names, numbers, money, price).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target grammar (Number + noun, "I want...", "Can I have...?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Speaks clearly and uses polite expressions ("please," "thank you").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborates well with peers during the role-play.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick oral or written exit ticket:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What fruit did I ask for today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I say 'please' and 'thank you'?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was fun about shopping today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult when I spoke in English?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The Market Role-Play was very engaging, and students actively used the target phrases for requesting items and expressing quantity. The pronunciation of numbers and target phonemes improved with modeling. Some students still need more practice with "Can I have...?" versus "I want...". I will provide more sentence frames and a quick review of the pragmatic differences in the next lesson's warm-up.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> Today we practiced speaking about shopping and fruit. In our next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition), we will write about what we want to buy at the store and create our own shopping lists using the vocabulary and grammar we practiced today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">2-3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>